### What is the Subsets Problem?

The subsets problem asks you to generate every possible combination of elements from a given set of distinct numbers, including the empty set and the full set. For n elements, there are exactly 2^n subsets. This is a foundational problem in the Subsets pattern family.

At National Bank of India, the product team wants to enumerate every possible add-on service bundle that a customer like Rajesh could choose from a list of available services.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetGenerator {
    public static List<List<Integer>> generateSubsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> subsets = new ArrayList<>();
        subsets.add(new ArrayList<>());  // start with empty subset

        for (int num : nums) {
            int size = subsets.size();  // snapshot before adding
            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                List<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<>(subsets.get(i));
                copy.add(num);
                subsets.add(copy);
            }
        }
        return subsets;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] serviceCodes = {101, 202, 303};  // overdraft, insurance, rewards
        List<List<Integer>> bundles = generateSubsets(serviceCodes);
        System.out.println("Total bundles: " + bundles.size());
        for (List<Integer> bundle : bundles) {
            System.out.println(bundle);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Total bundles: 8
[]
[101]
[202]
[101, 202]
[303]
[101, 303]
[202, 303]
[101, 202, 303]
```

### Why Does This Problem Matter?

Subset generation is the foundation for many algorithmic problems: combination sum, subset sum, power set queries, and feature toggle enumeration. Understanding it unlocks the entire Subsets pattern family used in backtracking and dynamic programming.

In banking, Priya at HDFC needs to evaluate every combination of loan adjustment options to find the best package for a customer. Subset generation gives her the complete search space.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.List;

public class LoanOptionEvaluator {
    public static void evaluateAllBundles(List<List<String>> bundles) {
        for (List<String> bundle : bundles) {
            System.out.println("Evaluating bundle: " + bundle);
        }
    }
}
```

### How Does the Iterative Doubling Technique Work?

The technique starts with a list containing only the empty subset. For each new element, it takes a snapshot of the current list size, then iterates over exactly that many existing subsets, copying each and appending the new element. This doubles the collection with each element.

The key detail is capturing the size before the round begins. If you iterated over the live collection while adding to it, you would process subsets you just created and over-generate.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class IterativeDoublingExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<List<String>> subsets = new ArrayList<>();
        subsets.add(new ArrayList<>());

        String[] services = {"Overdraft", "Insurance", "Rewards"};

        for (String service : services) {
            int snapshotSize = subsets.size();  // CRITICAL: snapshot before adding
            System.out.println("Processing " + service + ", current count: " + snapshotSize);
            for (int i = 0; i < snapshotSize; i++) {
                List<String> copy = new ArrayList<>(subsets.get(i));
                copy.add(service);
                subsets.add(copy);
            }
            System.out.println("After " + service + ", count doubled to: " + subsets.size());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Processing Overdraft, current count: 1
After Overdraft, count doubled to: 2
Processing Insurance, current count: 2
After Insurance, count doubled to: 4
Processing Rewards, current count: 4
After Rewards, count doubled to: 8
```

### How Is Iterative Doubling Different from Backtracking?

Both approaches produce the same result, but they differ in mechanism.

| Aspect | Iterative Doubling | Backtracking |
|---|---|---|
| Mechanism | Copy and extend existing subsets | Choose/unchoose elements recursively |
| Stack usage | No recursion, no stack growth | O(n) recursion depth |
| Code style | Loop-based, explicit list management | Recursive, implicit state on call stack |
| Duplicate handling | Requires sorting + skip for duplicates | Requires sorting + skip for duplicates |
| Ease of understanding | Concrete list manipulation | Abstract choose/explore/unchoose |

Backtracking version:

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetsBacktracking {
    public static List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
        backtrack(nums, 0, new ArrayList<>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void backtrack(int[] nums, int start, List<Integer> current, List<List<Integer>> result) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));  // record every partial subset
        for (int i = start; i < nums.length; i++) {
            current.add(nums[i]);
            backtrack(nums, i + 1, current, result);
            current.remove(current.size() - 1);  // unchoose
        }
    }
}
```

### What Are the Edge Cases?

The primary edge cases are an empty input array and an array with a single element. An empty array should produce exactly one subset: the empty set. A single-element array produces two subsets: the empty set and the set containing that one element.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Edge case 1: empty input
        int[] empty = {};
        List<List<Integer>> result1 = SubsetGenerator.generateSubsets(empty);
        System.out.println("Empty input subsets: " + result1);  // [[]]
        System.out.println("Count: " + result1.size());          // 1

        // Edge case 2: single element
        int[] single = {500};
        List<List<Integer>> result2 = SubsetGenerator.generateSubsets(single);
        System.out.println("Single element subsets: " + result2);  // [[], [500]]
        System.out.println("Count: " + result2.size());             // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Empty input subsets: [[]]
Count: 1
Single element subsets: [[], [500]]
Count: 2
```

### What Are the Limitations?

The output size is inherently exponential. For n elements there are 2^n subsets, so the total work is O(n * 2^n) counting the time to copy each subset. This means subset generation is only feasible for small n. For n = 20 there are about one million subsets, and for n = 30 there are about one billion.

In a banking context, if Rajesh has 25 optional services, enumerating all subsets produces over 33 million bundles, which is impractical for real-time display.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetFeasibility {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int n = 5; n <= 25; n += 5) {
            long count = 1L << n;  // 2^n
            System.out.println("n=" + n + " -> " + count + " subsets");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
n=5 -> 32 subsets
n=10 -> 1024 subsets
n=15 -> 32768 subsets
n=20 -> 1048576 subsets
n=25 -> 33554432 subsets
```

### What About Duplicate Elements?

When the input contains duplicate elements, the basic iterative doubling technique produces duplicate subsets. The fix is to sort the input first, and when processing a duplicate element, only extend the subsets that were added in the previous round rather than all existing subsets.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Arrays;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetsWithDuplicates {
    public static List<List<Integer>> subsetsWithDup(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);
        List<List<Integer>> subsets = new ArrayList<>();
        subsets.add(new ArrayList<>());
        int prevSize = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
            int start = (i > 0 && nums[i] == nums[i - 1]) ? prevSize : 0;
            int size = subsets.size();
            for (int j = start; j < size; j++) {
                List<Integer> copy = new ArrayList<>(subsets.get(j));
                copy.add(nums[i]);
                subsets.add(copy);
            }
            prevSize = size;
        }
        return subsets;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] codes = {100, 200, 200};  // duplicate service code
        System.out.println(subsetsWithDup(codes));
    }
}
```

```text
[[], [100], [200], [100, 200], [200, 200], [100, 200, 200]]
```

### What About Bit Manipulation?

An alternative approach uses bit manipulation. Each subset corresponds to a binary number from 0 to 2^n minus 1, where each bit indicates whether an element is included.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class SubsetsBitmask {
    public static List<List<Integer>> subsets(int[] nums) {
        int n = nums.length;
        int total = 1 << n;  // 2^n
        List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();

        for (int mask = 0; mask < total; mask++) {
            List<Integer> subset = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                if ((mask & (1 << i)) != 0) {
                    subset.add(nums[i]);
                }
            }
            result.add(subset);
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] amounts = {1000, 2000, 5000};
        System.out.println(subsets(amounts));
    }
}
```

```text
[[], [1000], [2000], [1000, 2000], [5000], [1000, 5000], [2000, 5000], [1000, 2000, 5000]]
```

### What Are Common Interview Follow-Up Questions?

Interviewers commonly ask these follow-ups after the basic subsets problem:

- Handle duplicate elements (Subsets II): sort and skip duplicates as shown above.
- Generate subsets of a fixed size K (Combinations): restrict the generation to subsets of exactly K elements.
- Find subsets that sum to a target (Subset Sum): combine subset generation with a sum constraint.
- Count subsets without generating them: the answer is always 2^n for distinct elements.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SubsetCount {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;  // 5 add-on services
        System.out.println("Total possible bundles: " + (1 << n));
    }
}
```

```text
Total possible bundles: 32
```
